{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YLambdaQSchur","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YLambdaQSchur","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Factorial.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical"],"declarations":34,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.dim_V_2_1_classical_eq_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.dim_V_2_1_classical_eq_two","kind":"theorem","line":574,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L574-L590","doc":"**Classical dimension matches q-dimension at q = 1.**\n\n`[2]_q` at q = 1 evaluates to `1 + 1 = 2` (handle the\nindeterminate `0/0` form via the equivalent `q + q⁻¹`\nexpression).\n\nSpecifically, `qInt q 2 = q + q⁻¹` for `q ≠ 0` and\n`q - q⁻¹ ≠ 0`. The classical-limit specialisation `q = 1` is\na degenerate case for this formula (since `q − q⁻¹ = 0`), but\nthe corresponding classical-dim statement is the hook-length\nresult `dim V_(2,1) = 2` proved above.\n\nStated over a generic field `{R} [Field R]` (§7c): `1 + 1 = 2`\nis an algebraic ring identity, not an archimedean one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.dim_V_2_1_eq_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.dim_V_2_1_eq_two","kind":"theorem","line":560,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L560-L572","doc":"**The hook-length formula at λ = (2,1) of S_3 gives dim = 2.**\n\nHook-length product for (2,1): 3 · 1 · 1 = 3.\nHence dim V_(2,1) = 3! / 3 = 6 / 3 = 2.\n\nThis is the elementary representation-theory content behind the\n\"2\" in the proton mass identity m_p = 2·m_μ·F_Bor − 12·m_e.\n\nProved by reflexive arithmetic; the natural-number factorial\nand division compute concretely. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qPow_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qPow_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":515,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L515-L517","doc":"`q^c|_{q=1} = 1` for any integer c. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_magnitude","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_magnitude","kind":"theorem","line":500,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L500-L508","doc":"**`|y_λ| = 1/[3]_q!` for every partition of 3 in q-Schur.**\n\nSquared form: `y_λ(q) · y_{λᵀ}(q) = 1/[3]_q!²` for the proton\nin H_3.  Stated as an explicit corollary specialised to n = 3. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_21_squared","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_21_squared","kind":"theorem","line":478,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L478-L487","doc":"**y_(2,1)² = 1/[3]_q!²** — the self-conjugate squared identity\nat H_3.  The (2,1) partition's y_λ squared equals the inverse\nof [3]_q! squared.\n\nAll three partitions of 3 thus satisfy `|y_λ| = 1/[3]_q!` in\nmagnitude — a structural fact (per\n`yLambdaQSchur_selfconjugate_squared`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_3_111_transpose_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_3_111_transpose_pair","kind":"theorem","line":470,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L470-L476","doc":"**y_(3) · y_(1,1,1) = 1/[3]_q!²** — the transpose-pair identity\nat the specific H_3 partitions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_21_explicit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_21_explicit","kind":"theorem","line":460,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L460-L468","doc":"**Explicit closed form of y_(2,1) at the proton**:\n`y_(2,1)(q) = 1 / [3]_q!`.\n\nContent sum = 0 makes the numerator `q^0 = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_111_explicit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_111_explicit","kind":"theorem","line":451,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L451-L458","doc":"**Explicit closed form of y_(1,1,1) at the proton**:\n`y_(1,1,1)(q) = q^{-3} / [3]_q!`.\n\nThe transpose pair of y_(3); content sum = -3. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_3_explicit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_3_explicit","kind":"theorem","line":440,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L440-L449","doc":"**Explicit closed form of y_(3) at the proton**:\n`y_(3)(q) = q^3 / [3]_q!`.\n\nBy definition.  Provides the explicit numerator for the\n\"trivial\" partition's y_λ contribution to the proton's\nMarkov trace. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_21_bar_sym","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_21_bar_sym","kind":"theorem","line":429,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L429-L438","doc":"**Bar-Mullineux at (2,1) self-conjugate** for the proton:\n`y_(2,1)(q⁻¹) = y_(2,1)(q)`.\n\nSpecialisation of `yLambdaQSchur_selfconjugate_bar_sym` with n=3.\nThe (2,1) partition is its own transpose, so bar acts as\nidentity on its y_λ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_bar_mullineux_3_to_111","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_proton_bar_mullineux_3_to_111","kind":"theorem","line":419,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L419-L427","doc":"**Bar-Mullineux at (3) ↔ (1,1,1)** for the proton:\n`y_(3)(q⁻¹) = y_(1,1,1)(q)`.\n\nSpecialisation of `yLambdaQSchur_bar_mullineux` with n=3, c=3\n(so λ=(3) has content sum 3, λᵀ=(1,1,1) has -3). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.F_Bor_eq_trM_over_y_3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.F_Bor_eq_trM_over_y_3","kind":"theorem","line":395,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L395-L407","doc":"**F_Bor identification**: F_Bor(q) = |tr_M(β_Bor, q)| / y_(3)(q)\nwhere y_(3)(q) = q^3 / [3]_q! is the q-Schur Markov weight for\nthe (3) partition.\n\nProof: trivial algebra\n  F_Bor := |tr_M| · [3]_q! / q^3\n         = |tr_M| / (q^3 / [3]_q!)\n         = |tr_M| / y_(3) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_three","kind":"theorem","line":355,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L355-L365","doc":"**`[3]_q! = (q + q⁻¹)(q² + 1 + q⁻²)`** — the explicit closed\nform of the q-factorial at n = 3 used by the proton in H_3(q).\n\nProof: chain `[3]_q!= [3]_q · [2]_q · [1]_q` with the just-\nproved `qInt_three` (gives `[3]_q`), `qFactorial_two` (gives\n`[2]_q!`), and `qFactorial_one` (gives `[1]_q! = 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_two","kind":"theorem","line":346,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L346-L353","doc":"**`[2]_q! = q + q⁻¹`**. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_three","kind":"theorem","line":330,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L330-L342","doc":"**`[3]_q = q² + 1 + q⁻²`** — the next non-trivial q-integer.\n\nUsed in `BorromeanMarkovTrace.lean` implicitly (the Borromean\n`tr_M` closed form's denominator is `[2]_q² = q² + 2 + q⁻²`,\none more than `[3]_q`).\n\nProof: `(q³ − q⁻³) = (q − q⁻¹)(q² + 1 + q⁻²)` by\nfactoring as a difference of cubes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_selfconjugate_bar_sym","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_selfconjugate_bar_sym","kind":"theorem","line":318,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L318-L326","doc":"**Self-conjugate partitions are bar-symmetric**: for `c = 0`,\n`y_λ(q⁻¹) = y_λ(q)`.\n\nTrivial corollary of `yLambdaQSchur_bar_mullineux` since\n`-c = 0 = c` when `c = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_bar_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_bar_self","kind":"theorem","line":302,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L302-L316","doc":"**Bar-self identity for y_λ**: y_λ(q) · y_λ(q⁻¹) = 1/[n]_q!².\n\nProof: by `yLambdaQSchur_bar_mullineux`, y_λ(q⁻¹) = y_{λᵀ}(q).\nMultiplying both sides by y_λ(q): y_λ(q) · y_λ(q⁻¹) =\ny_λ(q) · y_{λᵀ}(q) = 1/[n]_q!² by `yLambdaQSchur_transpose_pair`.\n\nStructurally: pairing the same partition with itself under bar\nyields the same product as pairing with its transpose under no\nbar — the framework's bar-Mullineux duality made explicit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_two_squared","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_two_squared","kind":"theorem","line":284,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L284-L293","doc":"**Corollary: `[2]_q² = q² + 2 + q⁻²`** — the Kauffman–Jones\nMarkov-trace normalisation, matching the convention used in\n`BorromeanMarkovTrace.lean` (where `tr_M(β_Bor) = V(q²)/[2]_q²`).\n\nThis is precisely the normalisation that gives the proved\n`trM_borromean h = (4 + h² − 3h⁴ − h⁶)/(h² + 4)` closed form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_two_eq_q_plus_q_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_two_eq_q_plus_q_inv","kind":"theorem","line":269,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L269-L282","doc":"**`[2]_q = q + q⁻¹`** — the simplest non-trivial q-integer identity.\n\nProof: `(q² − q⁻²) / (q − q⁻¹) = (q − q⁻¹)(q + q⁻¹) / (q − q⁻¹)\n= q + q⁻¹` by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares.\n\nRequires `q ≠ 0` (for q⁻¹ well-defined) and `q − q⁻¹ ≠ 0` (for\nthe q-integer denominator non-zero — equivalent to `q ≠ ±1` at\nthe substrate range `q > 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_one","kind":"theorem","line":251,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L251-L255","doc":"`[1]_q! = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_zero","kind":"theorem","line":247,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L247-L249","doc":"`[0]_q! = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_neg","kind":"theorem","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L238-L243","doc":"**q-integer anti-symmetry**: `[-n]_q = -[n]_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_one","kind":"theorem","line":232,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L232-L236","doc":"`[1]_q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_zero","kind":"theorem","line":226,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L226-L230","doc":"`[0]_q = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_bar_mullineux","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_bar_mullineux","kind":"theorem","line":213,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L213-L222","doc":"**Bar-Mullineux identity for y_λ**:  `y_λ(q⁻¹) = y_{λᵀ}(q)`\nin the q-Schur convention. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_bar_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial_bar_inv","kind":"theorem","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L194-L204","doc":"**q-factorial bar-symmetry**: `[n]_{q⁻¹}! = [n]_q!`.\n\nFollows from `qInt_bar_inv` by induction on `n : ℕ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_bar_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt_bar_inv","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L174-L192","doc":"**q-integer bar-symmetry**: `[n]_{q⁻¹} = [n]_q`.\n\nUnder bar (q ↔ q⁻¹), both numerator `q^n − q^{−n}` and denominator\n`q − q⁻¹` flip sign in the same way, so the ratio is invariant.\n\nThis is the fundamental bar-symmetry that makes [n]_q a\n\"balanced\" q-integer (vs the asymmetric `(q^n − 1)/(q − 1)`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_selfconjugate_squared","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_selfconjugate_squared","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L157-L164","doc":"For self-conjugate partitions (c = 0), y_λ(q)² = 1 / [n]_q!². ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_transpose_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur_transpose_pair","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L131-L148","doc":"**Transpose-pair identity.** For a partition λ ⊢ n with content\nsum `c`, and its transpose λ^T (which has content sum `−c`):\n\n  `y_λ(q) · y_{λ^T}(q) = 1 / [n]_q!²`.\n\nThe proof uses the fact that transposing a Young diagram\nnegates the content of every cell, so the content sums are\nnegatives of each other.  Combined with `qPow_mul_qPow_neg`\nthe q-dependence in the numerator cancels.\n\nThis is the cleanest non-trivial identity satisfied by the\nclosed-form y_λ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qPow_mul_qPow_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qPow_mul_qPow_neg","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L124-L129","doc":"`q^c · q^{−c} = 1` for any non-zero real `q` and any integer `c`.\n\nProof: `q^c · q^(-c) = q^(c + (-c)) = q^0 = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.q_sq_sub_one_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.q_sq_sub_one_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L112-L120","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.yLambdaQSchur","kind":"def","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L106-L109","doc":"Closed-form q-Schur Markov-trace weight:\n`y_λ(q) := q^{c(λ)} / [n]_q!`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qFactorial","kind":"def","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L88-L94","doc":"q-factorial `[n]_q! := [n]_q · [n−1]_q · … · [1]_q`.\n\nDefinition by recursion on `n : ℕ`:\n`[0]_q! = 1` (empty product), `[n+1]_q! = [n+1]_q · [n]_q!`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.qInt","kind":"def","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YLambdaQSchur.lean#L79-L86","doc":"Balanced q-integer at integer `n`:\n`[n]_q = (q^n - q^{-n}) / (q - q^{-1})`.\n\n`[0]_q = 0`, `[1]_q = 1`, `[2]_q = q + q⁻¹`, etc.\n\nConvention: `n : ℤ` so that `[-n]_q = -[n]_q`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}